{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.domain`\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` implements\n`trust_but_verify::domain::Domain` to define its node-identity type. Its\nerror type is introduced in {cite}`requirements.sdk.errors`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` has one obligation. The application domain implements `Domain` and defines its node-identity type as `type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. The cited `requirements.sdk.domain` and `requirements.sdk.errors` supply context and need no separate check here. The tests cover the obligation as follows.\n\n- **Bounds are not loosened.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]` contains a compile-time `check_bounds::<G: Domain>()` helper. It requires `G::NodeId` to satisfy `Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`. Dropping any of those four bounds from the trait would stop that generic code from compiling.\n- **Extra bounds are not added.** The same test implements `Domain` with an opaque `Identity(u8)` that derives only `Clone, PartialEq, Eq`. It has no `Debug`, `Hash` or `Ord`, and it is used for `node_path`, `outgoing_edges`, `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`. Adding a bound such as `Debug` or `Hash` to `NodeId` would stop the fixture from compiling. The test also compares the returned ids with `==`, which exercises `Eq`.\n- **A structured id works as a node identity.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/validation.rs#fn[enum_node_ids_distinguish_requirements_and_source_items_in_one_domain]` uses an enum `NodeId` with two variants. It asserts inequality, distinct paths, edges, dependency recording and evidence keyed by id.\n- **Real domains are exercised.** `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[habeas_paths_preserve_opaque_segments_and_separate_all_node_kinds]`, `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` and `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_delegates_unprepared_sources_and_paths_to_the_domain]` use `HabeasNode` as the `NodeId` in the real domain. The agent and criteria tests do the same with `Node(1)`.\n\nEach test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject. I found no obligation without an assertion or compile-time enforcement. The doc comment on `NodeId` is not testable behavior.",
  "references": "2e0816c1915fe94d28b5451c052712a0824feb8e7d2babaf5a1c18a378452fcc",
